   Since \(\mathcal{H}\) is an infinite-dimensional Hilbert space, it contains an infinite orthonormal basis \(\{e_i\}_{i=1}^\infty\). Each \(e_i\) is an element of \(\mathcal{H}\) and satisfies:
   \[
   \|e_i\| = 1 \quad \text{and} \quad \langle e_i, e_j \rangle = \delta_{ij}
   \]
   where \(\delta_{ij}\) is the Kronecker delta, which is 1 if \(i = j\) and 0 otherwise.

2. **Define Points in \(S\):**
   We define a set \(S\) consisting of points of the form:
   \[
   S = \left\{ \sum_{i=1}^n \alpha_i e_i : n \in \mathbb{N}, \alpha_i \in \mathbb{R} \right\}
   \]
   This means \(S\) includes all finite linear combinations of the orthonormal basis vectors.

3. **Verify Pairwise Distances:**
   Consider two distinct points \(x = \sum_{i=1}^{n_x} \alpha_i e_i\) and \(y = \sum_{j=1}^{n_y} \beta_j e_j\) in \(S\). Without loss of generality, assume \(n_x < n_y\) (the case \(n_y < n_x\) is symmetric).

   The difference vector between \(x\) and \(y\) is:
   \[
   x - y = \left( \sum_{i=1}^{n_x} \alpha_i e_i \right) - \left( \sum_{j=1}^{n_y} \beta_j e_j \right) = \sum_{i=1}^{n_x} \alpha_i e_i - \sum_{j=n_x+1}^{n_y} \beta_j e_j
